<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Widget color tokens — THE value table for both the live Glance widgets
  (via `WidgetColors`) and the static preview drawables/layouts, so the
  picker preview and the placed widget render identical hues.

  These used to be `@android:color/system_accent1_*` / `system_neutral*`
  references, i.e. Material You colours extracted from the user's wallpaper.
  That was the right call when the app itself was wallpaper-themed; it is the
  wrong one now. Open question 1 settled it: the app is one fixed dark palette,
  and a widget that follows the wallpaper instead puts two visual languages
  side by side on the same home screen. The wallpaper also cannot be held to
  the contrast ratios the palette is built on — `system_accent1_700` behind
  `system_accent1_100` is whatever the wallpaper makes of it.

  Values mirror `ui/theme/Color.kt` and must be kept in sync with it; the role
  names below map one-to-one onto the Material roles in `Theme.kt`. There is no
  `values-night` variant any more — the app is dark-only (decision 11), so a
  second table would only be a copy of this one.
-->
<resources>
    <!-- Background surface — `surfaceVariant` / AppSurfaceHigh. Deliberately
         NOT the app's #000000 canvas: a widget is a card on someone's
         wallpaper and needs an edge, which in-app comes from a hairline the
         widget has no way to draw. -->
    <color name="widget_surface">#FF141312</color>
    <color name="widget_on_surface">#FFEFE9E0</color>
    <color name="widget_on_surface_muted">#FFA79E92</color>

    <!-- Filled tonal surfaces — meal pills, the unfilled portion of GoalLeft. -->
    <color name="widget_primary_container">#FF123A2F</color>
    <color name="widget_on_primary_container">#FFB3EEDC</color>

    <!-- Strong call-to-action — the FAB, fully-saturated accents. -->
    <color name="widget_primary">#FF63D2B0</color>
    <color name="widget_on_primary">#FF00251B</color>

    <!-- Battery fill — AppPrimaryDeep, the one step between primaryContainer
         and primary. Was a flat 33%-black/white overlay, which on the new
         container reads as dirt rather than as a level. Solid instead of an
         alpha blend because the fill sits under the big number: a translucent
         fill changes that number's contrast as the day fills up. -->
    <color name="widget_primary_subtle">#FF1F6F5C</color>

    <!-- Meal pill — primaryContainer. The old night-mode deviation to a
         brighter `system_accent1_300` existed because the wallpaper-derived
         pill and the surface could land one step apart and collapse into each
         other. That cannot happen now: the mint #123A2F is separated from the
         neutral #141312 surface by hue, not only by a tone step the wallpaper
         chose. -->

    <!-- Recent food chip — neutral lift (surfaceContainerHighest), so it
         doesn't fight the meal pills for attention. -->

    <!-- Success / "just logged" tap feedback — `tertiary`. Was Material You
         `system_accent3`, picked as the wallpaper's complementary accent so it
         could never collide with the primary; tertiary is that same role in
         the fixed palette, and amber against mint is unmistakably not the
         resting state. -->
</resources>
